<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of South Vietnam</h1><p class="st-meta">1969 CE – 1976 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The provisional revolutionary government set up by the communist-led National Liberation Front in 1969, which formally ruled the south after Saigon's fall.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In June 1969 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">National Liberation Front</a> and allied groups formed the Provisional Revolutionary Government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-vietnam">South Vietnam</a> to give the southern insurgency a governmental form for negotiations. It was led by the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huynh-tan-phat">Huynh Tan Phat</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-huu-tho">Nguyen Huu Tho</a> as head of its advisory council.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The PRG operated from jungle bases near the Cambodian border and later in liberated zones, rather than a fixed capital. It was a party to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> peace talks, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-thi-binh">Nguyen Thi Binh</a> served as foreign minister and signed the 1973 accords. It was recognised by many communist and non-aligned states, though it was controlled by the Workers' Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975 the PRG became the nominal government of the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> moved quickly to reunification, and on 2 July 1976 it merged with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-south-vietnam.jpg" alt="Republic of South Vietnam" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_National_Liberation_Front_of_South_Vietnam,_Flag_of_South_Vietnam_(1975%E2%80%931976).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1969 CE – 1976 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Huynh Tan Phat, Nguyen Huu Tho, Nguyen Thi Binh</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Provisional Revolutionary Government of the Republic of South Vietnam, PRG</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=republic-of-south-vietnam" aria-label="Republic of South Vietnam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7618 1777 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of South Vietnam lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7938 2033L7939 2031M7938 2033L7846 1656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7939 2031h0M7846 1656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7938 2033h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7938 2033h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=republic-of-south-vietnam">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
